      <h1 class="essay-title">When Breath Becomes Air</h1>

      <p class="essay-subtitle">
        On mortality, meaning, and the difficult question of what makes a life well lived.
      </p>

      <p class="essay-meta">
        A personal reflection on Paul Kalanithi’s memoir
      </p>

      <hr class="essay-divider" />

      <div class="essay-body">
        <p>
          Some books leave you with answers. Others leave you with questions
          that follow you long after you have turned the final page.
          <em>When Breath Becomes Air</em> belongs to the second kind.
        </p>

        <p>
          Written by Paul Kalanithi, a neurosurgeon diagnosed with terminal
          lung cancer, the memoir explores a question that feels both simple
          and impossible to answer: how should we live when we know that our
          time is limited?
        </p>

        <p>
          What makes the book so moving is the way Kalanithi approaches this
          question from two sides. For much of his life, he studies death as
          a physician, encountering it through the lives of his patients.
          Then, suddenly, he must confront it as a patient himself. The
          distance between the person who treats an illness and the person
          who lives with it disappears.
        </p>

        <h2>When the future changes shape</h2>

        <p>
          We often organize our lives around the assumption that there will
          be more time. There will be another opportunity to change direction,
          another year to become the person we hope to be, another chance to
          say what we have been meaning to say.
        </p>

        <p>
          A serious illness disrupts that assumption. The future stops feeling
          like an endless landscape and becomes something uncertain and
          immediate. Plans that once seemed permanent may suddenly need to
          be reconsidered.
        </p>

        <p>
          Reading Kalanithi’s story made me think about how easily I confuse
          having time with knowing what to do with it. I can spend so much
          energy preparing for a future version of my life that I forget to
          pay attention to the one I am actually living.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          Perhaps the question is not simply how much time we have, but what
          we choose to give our time to.
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          This is not to say that every moment needs to be productive or
          extraordinary. A meaningful life does not have to be made up of
          constant achievements. Sometimes meaning is found in ordinary
          routines, in showing up for someone, in paying attention, or in
          allowing ourselves to be fully present.
        </p>

        <h2>The limits of achievement</h2>

        <p>
          Kalanithi was a person who had devoted years to becoming a skilled
          physician. His work required discipline, intelligence, and a
          willingness to spend much of his life preparing for a future goal.
          His diagnosis forced him to reconsider what those goals meant.
        </p>

        <p>
          I found myself thinking about the ways we measure a successful
          life. We often look at visible milestones: a degree, a career, a
          finished project, a place we have reached. These things can be
          meaningful, but they cannot answer every important question.
        </p>

        <p>
          What kind of person are we becoming while we pursue them? What
          relationships are we nurturing? Are we building a life that feels
          meaningful from the inside, or one that simply looks convincing
          from the outside?
        </p>

        <p>
          These questions do not make ambition less valuable. They make it
          more personal. They remind me that achievement is only one part of
          a life, not a substitute for living it.
        </p>

        <h2>Living with uncertainty</h2>

        <p>
          One of the hardest things about being human is that we rarely get
          complete certainty before we have to make our choices. We decide
          what matters without knowing exactly how things will unfold. We
          love people knowing that circumstances can change. We make plans
          without any guarantee that the future will cooperate.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is tempting to wait until life feels more settled before taking
          something seriously: a relationship, a creative idea, a difficult
          conversation, or a change we have been considering for years.
          But certainty is not always something we can wait for.
        </p>

        <p>
          Kalanithi’s story is a reminder that uncertainty is not an
          interruption to life. It has always been part of it, even when we
          are fortunate enough not to notice.
        </p>

        <p>
          We cannot control every circumstance, and we cannot always know
          which decisions will matter most in retrospect. We can, however,
          pay attention to the values that guide our choices and the people
          with whom we share our limited time.
        </p>

        <h2>What remains</h2>

        <p>
          I do not think a book like this needs to offer a neat lesson.
          Mortality is too personal, and grief is too complicated, for a
          single conclusion to explain them away.
        </p>

        <p>
          Instead, I take away a quieter reminder: that a meaningful life
          is not necessarily a perfect one, a long one, or one that follows
          the plan we imagined. It is a life in which we keep asking what
          matters, and try to bring our actions closer to our answers.
        </p>

        <p>
          We may not be able to choose how much time we are given. But we
          can reflect on how we spend it, what we pay attention to, and
          how we care for the people who make our lives what they are.
        </p>

        <p>
          Perhaps that is enough to begin with—not a final answer, but a
          more honest question to carry into the days ahead.
        </p>
